<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prithvi Narayan Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1723">1723</a>–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prithvi Narayan Shah was king of Gorkha from 1743 and the founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. He took Makwanpur in 1762 and blockaded and conquered the three kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, Kantipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, in 1768–1769, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> his capital. His sayings, the Dibya Upadesh, set out a policy of caution towards the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah.jpg" alt="Prithvi Narayan Shah" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: the Record · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prithvi_Narayan_Shah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
